 "post": {
  "title": "Why Doodlers Remember More #shorts",
  "description": "June doodles through the whole meeting, and she's the only one who remembers what was said. In a study by psychologist Jackie Andrade (University of Plymouth; Applied Cognitive Psychology, published online 2009), 40 people listened to a dull 2.5-minute phone message. The half who shaded shapes while listening recalled 29% more on a surprise memory test (7.5 vs 5.8 details). Andrade's explanation: doodling uses just enough attention to stop the mind drifting into daydreams. #psychology #shorts #memory #focus",
